 {"id":"QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-35","title":"[P3] ci-pass act leg discards each failing step's stdout: all three Python matrix jobs report Failure on 'Run tests (unit only, skip integration)' with zero captured output, so the act CI red is unattributable from the artifact","status":"pending","priority":"P3","created_at":"2026-11-13T10:52:21+00:00","created_by":null,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"capability_tags":[],"complexity":2,"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"description":null,"reasoning":null,"commit_hash":null,"worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":"Clean-machine battery tick terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06, agent 501380e4 @ bunker-mvp, payload bound to git HEAD f221e699c4ec9269a801fb56d1362ce4b49b5e38 (253 synced files verified byte-identical to that tree: 0 content diffs; the 1024 .gitreins/ and 1 .coding-hermes/ entries are harness metadata excluded by design). Evidence: qa-logs-2090793/ci.log (27161 bytes).","acceptance_criteria":"1) On a repo whose suite is red, the act leg of the clean-machine battery leaves the failing test name and the pytest summary line in the per-cell ci.log (or a per-job artifact); 2) today's artifact contains 0 occurrences of any test id and 0 'passed/failed' summary lines for the three matrix jobs; 3) rerun of the same battery on the same commit reproduces the attribution, not just the red.","updated_at":"2026-11-14T13:55:11+00:00","detail":{"fingerprint":"40c347e7ee23aeceea8bebc424163d5bb95c6b513573abf3922e39be3afac81d","evidence":[{"run_id":"terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06","ts":"2026-11-13T10:52:34+00:00","tree_fp":"caccfb77cbc063f27c2180b0ce0a03ac49af0753e6ba71d45ff0304b85e31567","artifacts":["/home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/qa-terminal-jail-2026-11-13-10-02-06/harvest/qa-logs-2090793/ci.log"]}]},"foreman_note":"Harness defect (NOT a product defect), reproduced this tick. ci.log shows all three matrix jobs as: Start Main Run tests (unit only, skip integration), immediately followed by Failure - Main Run tests [19m38s/19m46s/19m47s], then Job failed — no step stdout at all. grep -c test_install ci.log = 0; grep for passed/failed summary lines returns only generic job lines. So the act leg proves CI is red but cannot say WHY: native.log attributes it to plugin/test_install.py::test_successful_install, while act leaves the same failure unattributed. Consequence: every act-based ci-pass FAIL needs a manual docker-act rerun or gets mis-triaged as infrastructure. Side note: this same ci.log DOES carry setup-python errors for Lint/Audit/Drift (ModuleNotFoundError: No module named opcode; rm: cannot remove /opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.18/x64/lib/python3.13: Directory not empty) — that is the QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-14 provisioning class, annotated there, not here. Also: each matrix job spent ~19m40s in the step where the native leg needs ~49s — host contention at 6 concurrent batteries, context only.\n\nADOPTED-VERIFIED terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06: Artifacts preserved outside expiring scratch at /home/kara/.hermes/qa/harvest/terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06/qa-logs-2090793/ci.log (same bytes/fingerprint as original). Full ci.log also reports artifact-upload failures because ACTIONS_RUNTIME_TOKEN is absent; those are act-host limitations, not product failures. No native failure attribution is transferred to the opaque act matrix. Supplemental full-suite and lint outputs at /home/kara/.hermes/qa/harvest/terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06/qa-completion.\n\nREPRODUCED 2026-11-14 (tick terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-14-13-09-23, agent 7585507c @bunker-mvp): ci.log carries no FAILED/assert line for the three matrix jobs (Python 3.11-1 / 3.12-2 / 3.13-3); each dies at 'Run tests (unit only, skip integration)' after ~19 min with stdout discarded. Hosted CI on equivalent code is green (43600771735 @5a51e0f8 SUCCESS), so the act red is environment-side and its node cannot be named from the battery's own log - this row is what keeps ci-pass ungradeable."}
 {"id":"QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-36","title":"[P2] Kernel teardown verifier selects unbound global sleep-300 PIDs and aborts on cleanup EPERM","status":"pending","priority":"P2","created_at":"2026-11-13T11:21:19+00:00","created_by":null,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"capability_tags":[],"complexity":3,"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"description":null,"reasoning":"Clean-machine full-suite completion on existing JIT agent 501380e4 at bunker-mvp, product tree f221e699. Cell token full-suite-completion/kernel-matrix. Full unfiltered pytest: 11 failed, 2033 passed, 65 skipped in 253.72s. tests/test_kernel_matrix_teardown.py::test_harness_human_mode_names_the_host_condition crashes at scripts/kernel-matrix-teardown.py:315 os.kill(payload, SIGKILL): PermissionError EPERM. Source _sleep300_pids():200-211 scans ALL visible /proc sleep 300 commands; run_backend_cell():268-270 chooses min(new), with no UID/ancestry check, then cleanup signals that PID. The observed EPERM is reproduced by this battery; the precise selected PID was not captured, so foreign ownership is a source-backed risk rather than a measured PID attribution. This is a product verification-script robustness/safety defect, not proof of jail containment failure. Evidence: /home/kara/.hermes/qa/harvest/terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06/qa-completion/full-suite.log.","commit_hash":null,"worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"acceptance_criteria":"Use a deterministic regression with concurrent unrelated sleep-300-shaped processes and a launcher whose payload is unavailable: the verifier never adopts or signals an unrelated PID. Bind payload discovery to launcher descendants or a per-run identity, verify ownership before cleanup, and classify cleanup EPERM as structured evidence instead of aborting JSON/human output. Run the full pytest suite without -x or selectors; report unsupported namespace prerequisites explicitly.","updated_at":"2026-11-13T11:23:15+00:00","detail":{"fingerprint":"7184c0c8ef76b61a44148987022761b370dfdfdc7a4e002fbe902b8d5363636e","evidence":[{"run_id":"terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06","ts":"2026-11-13T11:21:19+00:00","tree_fp":"26c9e8540a2cab6a8e8a15a4015f72f2eb5b306b07a78d3eb1a9240e01161ff4","artifacts":["/home/kara/.hermes/qa/harvest/terminal-jail-qa-2026-11-13-10-02-06/qa-completion/full-suite.log"]}]},"foreman_note":null}
-{"id":"TJ-DF-066","title":"[P2] A user rule with an unknown match.type loads silently and never fires — no warning, and rules-drift-probe still reports it as a healthy extension rule","status":"pending","priority":"P2","created_at":"2026-11-14T10:20:00-05:00","created_by":"dogfood-tick-2026-11-14-10-01-04","depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["rules","interruptor","trust","dogfood"],"complexity":3,"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"description":"Dogfood tick 2026-11-14-10-01-04 drove the documented deny-by-default path (docs/quickstart.md:543 — add a catch-all block rule under ~/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/). Two of the three surfaces behave correctly; the third fails silently. POSITIVE (no action): the documented catch-all works exactly as written — a priority-1 pattern '.' block rule denies everything the builtin allow rules do not match (`date` -> block/user-deny-by-default, rc 126) while builtin allows still win at priority 500 (`echo hello` -> allow-echo, `ls /tmp` -> allow-ls). POSITIVE: a schema-invalid rule FILE fails CLOSED and says why ('REFUSING rule file <path>: rule #1 (<id>): field priority must be an integer, got str (critical) ... fail-closed: blocking command (enforce mode)', rc 126). POSITIVE: unknown ACTION is now surfaced with rule_id + reason (the QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-30 fix, commit 9020095, verified live at HEAD). DEFECT: an unknown match.TYPE is silently swallowed. Fixture /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/tj-dogfood-20261114/usr-regex/rules.d/90-user.yaml contains an otherwise-valid rule `id: user-block-regex-type, priority: 900, action: block, match: {type: regex, regex: '^date$'}` — 'regex' is a natural guess because `regex` is a documented match FIELD name in the same block (rules.py:187-210 accepts `regex` as a key) — and the identical rule with `type: pattern` blocks. Measured A/B, same dir, same command (`./standalone/terminal-jail date`): type=pattern -> BLOCKED rc 126 (rule_id user-block-pattern-type); type=regex -> the command RUNS, rc 0, verdict rule_id null, and stderr carries no warning of any kind. Cause: matcher.py:157-159, `handler = dispatch.get(match_type); if handler is None: return MatchResult()` — an unknown type is treated as 'does not match', and rules.py:183-184 says so in a source comment ('unknown match.type is a no-op today ... so only the TYPES of the keys present are validated here'). The silence is the harm: a user who writes a protective rule believes it is armed, and the one operator surface that inspects user rules blesses it — `python3 scripts/rules-drift-probe.py` over the same dir prints 'RESULT: drift=0 overridden=0 corrupt=0 unoverridden=55 non-builtin=1' and lists 'INFO: non-builtin rule id(s) present (extension mechanism, never drift): user-block-regex-type'. Suggested shape (owner's call): mirror the 9020095 treatment — emit one stderr WARNING naming the rule id and the unknown type plus the accepted vocabulary, and make rules-drift-probe flag a non-builtin rule whose match.type has no handler, so the rule cannot look healthy while being dead. Docs half: quickstart.md:543 never names the match.type vocabulary nor points at `python3 scripts/rule-pack-tool.py schema`, which does enumerate all nine types (pattern/path/composite/syscall/network/heredoc/subcommand + args form) — TJ-DF-035 fixed that discoverability for rule PACKS only, and the user-rule path the quickstart recommends got none of it.","acceptance_criteria":"1) A user rule whose match.type is not in the accepted vocabulary produces exactly one stderr WARNING naming the rule id, the offending type, and the accepted types, on the same surface that already reports an unknown action (mirror the 9020095 treatment) — silence is the defect, not the no-op itself.\n2) scripts/rules-drift-probe.py reports such a rule as problematic (drift/corrupt or an explicit flag), not as a healthy \"non-builtin extension rule\"; the operator surface that inspects user rules must not bless a rule that can never fire.\n3) Behaviour for valid rules is unchanged: with the same fixture dir, `type: pattern` still blocks (rc 126, rule_id user-block-pattern-type) and builtin allows still win at priority 500.\n4) Closeout evidence: the A/B run at the fix commit — type=pattern vs type=regex, each with its rc, verdict rule_id and full stderr — plus rules-drift-probe output before/after showing the rule no longer counted as healthy."}
