 {"id":"TJ-DF-067","title":"[P3] docs/quarterly-review.md §3 CLI checklist cannot pass on a working build: all 5 command lines are stale (`--` terminator unsupported, `--pid`/`--fork` flags do not exist) and nothing executes docs outside README.md","status":"pending","priority":"P3","created_at":"2026-11-14T11:09:34+00:00","created_by":null,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["docs","trust","cli","dogfood"],"complexity":2,"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"description":null,"reasoning":"Dogfood tick 2026-11-14-10-01-04 (fire 4) walked the maintainer's own quarterly/release verification checklist at HEAD 8fb5e4b (clean product tree, CI green on that exact commit) and found its Standalone-CLI section (docs/quarterly-review.md:37-43) unable to pass on a build that works correctly:\n- `standalone/terminal-jail -- echo hello` -> rc 127, \"terminal-jail: --: command not found\". The wrapper has no end-of-options terminator: the first non-flag argument IS the command, so `--` is exec'd as a program name.\n- `standalone/terminal-jail --user --pid --fork -- echo hello` -> rc 127, \"--pid: command not found\". `--pid` and `--fork` are not flags of this CLI at all: --help lists only --user, --seccomp, --interruptor, --no-interruptor, --private-proc, --help, --version. So that line documents two flags that do not exist and one terminator that does not exist.\n- `standalone/terminal-jail --seccomp -- echo hello` -> rc 1 with an uncaught FileNotFoundError traceback (the code half is filed separately as TJ-DF-068: the seccomp loader's own usage at standalone/seccomp-loader.py:76 promises \"[--] <command>\" but never consumes it).\nPOSITIVE, no action: the two checklist lines that do not use `--` are accurate today — `--help` prints usage, `--version` prints 1.3.3, and `--seccomp /bin/echo SECCOMP-OK` runs and returns rc 0.\nWHY IT MATTERS: this file is the checklist a maintainer runs before declaring a release verified. Five of five CLI checks fail on a healthy build, so the operator either files phantom regressions against working code or learns to skip section 3 — which is how the section stayed stale in the first place. PROVENANCE: the file was last touched by DOC-3 (commit 1976e17, 2026-09-28), which repaired one nonexistent test-file line ABOVE this section and never executed the CLI block; docs/quarterly-review.md is also outside every automated guard — scripts/docs-check.py checks fenced-block balance plus a pinned argv list in README.md only (its header states \"Nothing else in CI reads README.md\", docs-check.py:4), and no script parses docs/quarterly-review.md.\nDISTINCT FROM: DOC-3 (complete) covered the §2 test-file reference only; TJ-DF-068 covers the loader's argv/exit-status behaviour. This row is the doc-truth + guard gap.","acceptance_criteria":"1) Every command line in docs/quarterly-review.md section 3 either runs verbatim on a supported host (rc 0) or is rewritten to the invocation that actually does what the line claims: remove the unsupported `--` terminator and the nonexistent `--pid`/`--fork` flags from all five lines, naming the real isolation flags from --help.\n2) The corrected lines are EXECUTED by an automated check in CI rather than eyeballed: extend the pinned-command list in scripts/docs-check.py (the commands table at docs-check.py:61-78) or add an equivalent parser so that docs/quarterly-review.md's checked commands run, and a future flag rename fails the build instead of rotting the checklist.\n3) Closeout evidence: the new check's output showing each section-3 line executed with its rc (or an explicit, documented skip reason for host-dependent lines such as the namespace/seccomp probes), plus a negative control showing that deliberately breaking one line makes the check fail.","updated_at":"2026-11-14T11:09:34+00:00","detail":{"fingerprint":"6c19669ebee2a3304bfc75a9931df0a25cb3358373fa9bbf82ae7600907e7c7b"}}
 {"id":"TJ-DF-068","title":"[P3] --seccomp turns a missing command into a raw Python traceback with exit 1 (every other path: clean rc 127) and ignores the `--` terminator its own usage promises","status":"pending","priority":"P3","created_at":"2026-11-14T11:09:58+00:00","created_by":null,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["cli","seccomp","errors","trust","dogfood"],"complexity":3,"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"description":null,"reasoning":"Dogfood tick 2026-11-14-10-01-04 (fire 4) at HEAD 8fb5e4b. The seccomp launch path handles a missing command differently from every other path, and it does not honour the `--` terminator its own usage string promises.\nMEASURED, same checkout, same missing binary (/nope/definitely-not-a-cmd), only flags varied:\n  (no flags)        -> rc 127, one clean line: \"terminal-jail: /nope/...: command not found\"\n  --user            -> rc 127, clean\n  --seccomp         -> rc 1, uncaught traceback from standalone/seccomp-loader.py:77 os.execvp(args[0], args) -> FileNotFoundError\n  --user --seccomp  -> rc 1, same traceback\nIdentical result for a bare-name typo (`--seccomp definitely-not-a-cmd`) and for `--seccomp -- echo hello`, which is exactly the line docs/quarterly-review.md:41 tells a maintainer to run.\nTWO DEFECTS IN ONE FUNCTION (standalone/seccomp-loader.py:69-77):\n(a) ARGV — the usage it prints is \"Usage: seccomp-loader.py [--] <command> [args...]\" but the code does `args = sys.argv[1:]` and never consumes a leading `--`, so `--` is exec'd as the program name.\n(b) ERROR HANDLING — execvp is unguarded, so a missing command escapes as a Python traceback and Python's default exit 1, where the rest of the product returns one clean line and 127. specs/cli.md:373 states the contract this breaks: exit statuses from the launch path are pass-through and 2 is reserved for wrapper pre-flight failures — a traceback's 1 is neither, so a caller that distinguishes \"command not found\" (127) from \"the jail refused me\" (2/126) is told a typo was an internal failure. To a human a traceback reads as a bug in the tool rather than in the command, the opposite of the readable-refusal standard the rules engine already meets (REFUSING rule file ... / user-deny-by-default, verified in this same tick).\nSUGGESTED SHAPE (owner's call): consume one leading `--`, then wrap the exec in try/except (FileNotFoundError -> 127, PermissionError/IsADirectoryError/NotADirectoryError -> 126) emitting a single stderr line naming the command and the errno, mirroring the wrapper's existing message text.","acceptance_criteria":"1) `./standalone/terminal-jail --seccomp -- /bin/echo hi` prints hi and exits 0 — the `[--]` in the loader's own usage string is implemented, not merely documented.\n2) `./standalone/terminal-jail --seccomp /nope/definitely-not-a-cmd` emits exactly one diagnostic line naming the path, no Python traceback, and exits 127, matching the no-seccomp path for the same input; a non-executable target exits 126.\n3) Holds identically for `--user --seccomp` and for a bare-name typo such as `--seccomp definitely-not-a-cmd`.\n4) Tests cover both halves: a loader argv test (one leading `--` stripped, a lone `--` is a usage error) and an exec-failure test asserting the message shape AND the exit status with no traceback text.\n5) Closeout evidence: the four commands above run live at the fix commit with their rc, plus the names of the tests that pin them.","updated_at":"2026-11-14T11:09:58+00:00","detail":{"fingerprint":"34339c0cfcc445a425bdfa59efd7d9baf8b6308ffd0608aff1cc3e84ba2aa427"}}
 {"id":"TJ-REVIEW-074-R2","title":"[P2] Live recurrence of TJ-REVIEW-074's promise at HEAD 8aad6df: the auto-sandbox (modify) interpreter preflight still passes a python3 the filesystem tier cannot reach, so the payload dies with a bare 'bash: Permission denied', rc=126, and NO named 'auto-sandbox modify unavailable' verdict","status":"pending","priority":"P2","created_at":"2026-11-14T12:05:00-05:00","created_by":"dogfood-tick-terminal-jail-dogfood-2026-11-14-10-01-04","depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["modify-path","auto-sandbox","landlock","preflight","diagnosability","dogfood"],"complexity":4,"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"description":"DOGFOOD TICK terminal-jail-dogfood-2026-11-14-10-01-04 (fire 5, real use, HEAD 8aad6df, checkout binary standalone/terminal-jail, host kernel 7.0.0-38-generic, load ~21, TERMINAL_JAIL_* unset).\nTJ-REVIEW-074 was closed complete on the promise written into its own evidence: 'plugin/test_modify_preflight.py::test_modify_rewrite_interpreter_denial_is_named' — 'A rewrite must preflight its interpreter, not leak rc=126 from bash' (plugin/test_modify_preflight.py:291-315). Live, on a default launch, the leak is back:\n  cd <dir with `ok: @echo PAYLOAD-RAN`>; terminal-jail make -f Makefile.ok ok\n  -> rc=126, stdout empty, stderr = ONE structured verdict event {\"event\":\"verdict\",\"action\":\"modify\",\"rule_id\":\"auto-make\",\"reason\":\"Command modified by auto-sandbox\"} followed by two human lines, the second being a bare\n  bash: line 1: /home/kara/.hermes/hermes-agent/venv/bin/python3: Permission denied\nNo line on any surface says 'auto-sandbox modify unavailable', 'command not run', or names the interpreter as the cause — the named-cause machinery at standalone/terminal-jail:1267 (and the :1280/:1294 siblings) exists in this very tree and stays silent on this shape.\nCAUSE, isolated by a 2x2x3 probe matrix (fire5 probe11/matrix/probe12, all read-only): the existence of the filesystem tier is the switch, not the cwd or the binary.\n  A checkout binary, tier ON (default), cwd outside checkout -> rc=126, Permission denied on the venv python3.\n  B same, TERMINAL_JAIL_LANDLOCK=0                                     -> rc=0, PAYLOAD-RAN.\n  C same, TERMINAL_JAIL_PYTHON=/usr/bin/python3 (tier ON)             -> still rc=126, SAME venv path in the error: the pin is ignored on the modify rewrite (this half is already owned by PENDING RELEASE-TJ-021).\n  E installed v1.3.3 binary, same cwd                               -> rc=0 PAYLOAD-RAN, because ITS loader path is unreadable and the tier silently degrades instead.\n  F why: /home/kara is drwxr-x--x (traversable) but /home/kara/.hermes is 0700, and the hermes venv python3 lives under it; /usr/bin/python3 is root:root and readable. The rewrite's preflight resolves the interpreter BEFORE the Landlock domain is installed (the tier branch is at standalone/terminal-jail:1449-1487, the named verdicts at :1267/:1280/:1294), so 'the interpreter resolves' passes and the exec then hits the tier.\n  Note the pinned test cannot catch this: it stubs unshare and denies via a 0644 shebang target, i.e. it exercises the pre-tier denial shape only; a tier-installed denial of an otherwise executable interpreter is a different ordering and is untested.\nRELATED (do not duplicate): TJ-DF-050 = same class for the NET LOADER on the modify path (closed); RELEASE-TJ-021 = the trampolines hardcode python3 and ignore TERMINAL_JAIL_PYTHON (PENDING) — cell C above is live evidence for it; TJ-REVIEW-074 = the original row this recurs.\nIMPACT judged as a user: on any host whose PATH python3 sits under a 0700 home (every dev box that puts a venv python3 first, this one included) the auto-sandbox build path is unusable, and the failure names neither the sandbox nor the interpreter.","acceptance_criteria":"1) With the filesystem tier ON and PATH's python3 under a directory the payload uid cannot traverse, `terminal-jail make -f Makefile.ok ok` either runs the payload or emits exactly one named line 'terminal-jail: auto-sandbox modify unavailable (...)' that names the interpreter path and states the command was not run — a bare 'bash: ... Permission denied' plus rc=126 must never be the only signal, and the verdict event must not read as a plain 'Command modified by auto-sandbox' success.\n2) A regression test drives the tier-ON shape end to end (real landlock-loader with a real unreadable interpreter under a 0700 ancestor), not only the stubbed pre-tier denial at plugin/test_modify_preflight.py:291; that test must FAIL on HEAD 8aad6df and pass after the fix.\n3) With the tier ON and TERMINAL_JAIL_PYTHON set to a readable interpreter, the same command runs the payload (rc=0, PAYLOAD-RAN) — closing the pin half jointly with RELEASE-TJ-021.\n4) README 'Graceful Degradation' documents the tier-ON modify-path failure mode and the workaround (TERMINAL_JAIL_LANDLOCK=0) next to the existing tier overhead note."}
